  "result": "Excellent! All tests pass successfully. Here's a summary of the dashboard implementation:\n\n## Dashboard Implementation Summary\n\n**Added to `src/reports.js`:**\n\n`dashboard(invoices, asOf)` function that returns:\n- **`counts`**: Number of invoices per status (draft, issued, partially_paid, paid)\n- **`outstanding`**: Total amount still due on invoices with status 'issued' or 'partially_paid'\n- **`overdue`**: Object with count and amount of overdue invoices from `overdueInvoices()`\n- **`topCustomers`**: Array of top 3 customers with most outstanding, sorted by due amount (highest first), as `[{ customerId, due }, ...]`\n\n**Key Features:**\n- Validates the asOf date\n- Aggregates multiple invoices per customer for topCustomers\n- Excludes draft and paid invoices from outstanding and topCustomers\n- Properly handles partially paid invoices\n\n**Tests Added** (13 tests in `test/reports.test.js`):\n1. Date validation\n2. Empty invoice list handling\n3. Invoice counting by status\n4. Outstanding calculation (issued + partially_paid)\n5. Exclusion of draft and paid from outstanding\n6. Overdue count and amount\n7. Top 3 customers with most due\n8. Sorting by due amount (highest first)\n9. Aggregation of multiple invoices per customer\n10. Exclusion of draft invoices from topCustomers\n11. Exclusion of paid invoices from topCustomers\n12. Limit to 3 customers even with more\n13. Partial payment handling\n\n**Test Results:**\n- 365 total tests pass\n- 13 new dashboard tests all pass\n- No breaking changes to existing functionality",
